fr:mainmatter.fr:p:
Let K be a DG mixed Hodge A-complex.
With the notation of , , and , (\mathrm {s} K,\delta (W,L),F) is a mixed Hodge A-complex.
We have
1268hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.1hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.1.xmlEquation8.1.15.1 {}_{\delta (W,L)}E_1^{ab}(\mathrm {s} K\otimes \mathbb {Q}) = \sum _{\substack {\alpha =\gamma -\beta \\b=\alpha +\beta }} \operatorname {H}^\alpha (\operatorname {Gr}_\beta ^W K^{\bullet \gamma }) \tag{8.1.15.1}
where the complex ({}_{\delta (W,L)}E_1^{\bullet b},d_1) is the simple complex associated to the double complex of Hodge A\otimes \mathbb {Q}-structures of weight b given by
1269hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.2hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.2.xmlEquation8.1.15.2 \small \begin {CD} \operatorname {H}^{b-(\beta +1)}(\operatorname {Gr}_{\beta +1}^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma +1}) @>{\partial }>> \operatorname {H}^{b-\beta }(\operatorname {Gr}_\beta ^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma +1}) @>{\partial }>> \operatorname {H}^{b-(\beta -1)}(\operatorname {Gr}_{\beta -1}^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma +1}) \\@. @A{d''}AA @AA{d''}A \\@. \operatorname {H}^{b-\beta }(\operatorname {Gr}_\beta ^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma }) @>>{\partial }> \operatorname {H}^{b-(\beta -1)}(\operatorname {Gr}_{\beta -1}^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma }) \end {CD} \tag{8.1.15.2}
On the {}_LE_r pages of the spectral sequence defined by (\mathrm {s} K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},L), the recurrent filtration and the two direct filtrations defined by \operatorname {Dec}_1(W) agree; similarly, on the {}_LE_r(\mathrm {s} K_\mathbb {C}), the recurrent filtration and the two direct filtrations induced by F agree. We denoted these filtrations again by W and F (respectively).
For r\geqslant 1, ({}_LE_r,W,F) is a mixed Hodge A-structure (). The differentials d_r are morphisms of mixed Hodge A-structures.
The filtration L on \operatorname {H}^\bullet (\mathrm {s} K) is a filtration in the category of mixed Hodge structures, and
1270hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.3hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.3.xmlEquation8.1.15.3 \operatorname {Gr}_L^p\big ( \operatorname {H}^{p+q}(\mathrm {s} K), \delta (W,L)[p+q], F \big ) = ({}_LE_\infty ^{pq},W,F). \tag{8.1.15.3}

Let A be as in . Then a Hodge A-complex K of weight n consists of
a complex K_A\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+(A) such that \operatorname {H}^k(K_A) is an A-module of finite type for all k;
a filtration F on K_A\otimes \mathbb {C}, i.e. a filtered complex (K_\mathbb {C},F)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F(\mathbb {C}), and an isomorphism \alpha \colon K_\mathbb {C}\simeq K_A\otimes \mathbb {C} in \mathrm {D}^+(\mathbb {C}).

A mixed Hodge A-complex K consists of:






        a complex K_A\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+(A) such that \operatorname {H}^k(K_A) is an A-module of finite type for all k;






        a filtered complex (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F(A\otimes \mathbb {Q}) (for an increasing filtration W) and an isomorphism K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}}\simeq  K_A\otimes \mathbb {Q} in \mathrm {D}^+(A\otimes \mathbb {Q});






        a bifiltered complex (K_\mathbb {C},W,F)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F_w(\mathbb {C}) (for an increasing filtration W and a decreasing filtration F) and an isomorphism \alpha \colon (K_\mathbb {C},W)\simeq (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\otimes \mathbb {C} in \mathrm {D}^+F(\mathbb {C}).

A cohomological mixed Hodge A-complex K on a topological space X consists of:






        a complex K_A\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+(X,A) such that \operatorname {\mathbb {H}}^k(X,K_A) is an A-module of finite type for all k, and such that \operatorname {\mathbb {H}}(X,K_A)\otimes \mathbb {Q}\xrightarrow {\sim }\operatorname {\mathbb {H}}(X,K_A\otimes \mathbb {Q});






        a filtered complex (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F(X,A\otimes \mathbb {Q}) (for an increasing filtration W) and an isomorphism K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}}\simeq  K_A\otimes \mathbb {Q} in \mathrm {D}^+(X,A\otimes \mathbb {Q});






        a bifiltered complex (K_\mathbb {C},W,F)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F_w(X,\mathbb {C}) (for an increasing filtration W and a decreasing filtration F) and an isomorphism \alpha \colon (K_\mathbb {C},W)\simeq (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\otimes \mathbb {C} in \mathrm {D}^+F(X,\mathbb {C}).

A DG mixed Hodge A-complex K consists of:






        a complex K_A\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+(A\mathsf {-mod}_{\mathsf {DG}^+});





        a filtered complex (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F((A\otimes \mathbb {Q})\mathsf {-mod}_{\mathsf {DG}^+}), and is isomorphism K_A\otimes \mathbb {Q}\simeq  K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}} in \mathrm {D}^+;





        a bifiltered complex (K_\mathbb {C},F,W)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F_2(\mathbb {C}\mathsf {-mod}_{\mathsf {DG}^+}), and an isomorphism K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}}\otimes \mathbb {C}\simeq  K_\mathbb {C} in \mathrm {D}^+F.

Let X_\bullet  be a simplicial topological space.
    A cohomological mixed Hodge A-complex K on X_\bullet  consists of:






        a complex K_A\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+(X_\bullet ,A);






        a filtered complex (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F(X_\bullet ,A\otimes \mathbb {Q}), and an isomorphism K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}}\simeq  K_A\otimes \mathbb {Q} in \mathrm {D}^+(X_\bullet ,A\otimes \mathbb {Q});






        a bifiltered complex (K_\mathbb {C},W,F)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F_2(X_\bullet ,\mathbb {C}), and an isomorphism (K_\mathbb {C},W)\simeq (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\otimes \mathbb {C} in \mathrm {D}^+F(X_\bullet ,\mathbb {C}).

Let K be a cohomological mixed Hodge A-complex on X_\bullet .
    Write A\mathsf {-mod}_\mathsf {cosimp} to mean the category of cosimplicial A-modules.
    If we apply the functor \mathrm {R}\Gamma ^\bullet  to K, then we obtain






        a cosimplicial complex \mathrm {R}\Gamma ^\bullet  K_A\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+(A\mathsf {-mod}_\mathsf {cosimp});






        a cosimplicial filtered complex \mathrm {R}\Gamma ^\bullet (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F(A\otimes \mathbb {Q}\mathsf {-mod}_\mathsf {cosimp});






        a cosimplicial bifiltered complex \mathrm {R}\Gamma ^\bullet (K_\mathbb {C},W,F)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F_2(\mathbb {C}\mathsf {-mod}_\mathsf {cosimp});






        isomorphisms as in (b) and (c) of  between these objects.

Let K be a DG mixed Hodge A-complex.
With the notation of , , and , (\mathrm {s} K,\delta (W,L),F) is a mixed Hodge A-complex.
We have
1268hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.1hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.1.xmlEquation8.1.15.1 {}_{\delta (W,L)}E_1^{ab}(\mathrm {s} K\otimes \mathbb {Q}) = \sum _{\substack {\alpha =\gamma -\beta \\b=\alpha +\beta }} \operatorname {H}^\alpha (\operatorname {Gr}_\beta ^W K^{\bullet \gamma }) \tag{8.1.15.1}
where the complex ({}_{\delta (W,L)}E_1^{\bullet b},d_1) is the simple complex associated to the double complex of Hodge A\otimes \mathbb {Q}-structures of weight b given by
1269hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.2hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.2.xmlEquation8.1.15.2 \small \begin {CD} \operatorname {H}^{b-(\beta +1)}(\operatorname {Gr}_{\beta +1}^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma +1}) @>{\partial }>> \operatorname {H}^{b-\beta }(\operatorname {Gr}_\beta ^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma +1}) @>{\partial }>> \operatorname {H}^{b-(\beta -1)}(\operatorname {Gr}_{\beta -1}^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma +1}) \\@. @A{d''}AA @AA{d''}A \\@. \operatorname {H}^{b-\beta }(\operatorname {Gr}_\beta ^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma }) @>>{\partial }> \operatorname {H}^{b-(\beta -1)}(\operatorname {Gr}_{\beta -1}^W K^{\bullet ,\gamma }) \end {CD} \tag{8.1.15.2}
On the {}_LE_r pages of the spectral sequence defined by (\mathrm {s} K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},L), the recurrent filtration and the two direct filtrations defined by \operatorname {Dec}_1(W) agree; similarly, on the {}_LE_r(\mathrm {s} K_\mathbb {C}), the recurrent filtration and the two direct filtrations induced by F agree. We denoted these filtrations again by W and F (respectively).
For r\geqslant 1, ({}_LE_r,W,F) is a mixed Hodge A-structure (). The differentials d_r are morphisms of mixed Hodge A-structures.
The filtration L on \operatorname {H}^\bullet (\mathrm {s} K) is a filtration in the category of mixed Hodge structures, and
1270hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.3hodge-theory-iii-8.1.15.3.xmlEquation8.1.15.3 \operatorname {Gr}_L^p\big ( \operatorname {H}^{p+q}(\mathrm {s} K), \delta (W,L)[p+q], F \big ) = ({}_LE_\infty ^{pq},W,F). \tag{8.1.15.3}

In , the filtration \operatorname {Dec}_1(W) () plays a more important role than W. We have
1272hodge-theory-iii-8.1.16.1hodge-theory-iii-8.1.16.1.xmlEquation8.1.16.1 \operatorname {Dec}(\delta (W,L)) = \operatorname {Dec}_1(W) \qquad \text {on }\mathrm {s} K \tag{8.1.16.1}
and can be rewritten as
1273hodge-theory-iii-8.1.16.2hodge-theory-iii-8.1.16.2.xmlEquation8.1.16.2 \operatorname {Gr}_L(\operatorname {H}(K),\operatorname {Dec}_1(W,F)) = ({}_LE_\infty ,W,F). \tag{8.1.16.2}
In the same spirit, we note that, with the notation of , \mathrm {R}\Gamma \big ( \mathrm {R} j_*\mathbb {Z}, (\mathrm {R} j_*\mathbb {Q},\operatorname {Dec} W), (\Omega _X^\bullet (\log D),\operatorname {Dec} W,F) \big ) depends only on U, and not on the choice of compactification X.

html:ol.html:li:
The filtration \delta (W,L) of \mathrm {R}\Gamma \mathrm {R} j_*\mathbb {Q}\simeq \mathrm {R}\Gamma (U_\bullet ,\mathbb {Q}).
With the notation of , we have
         \begin {aligned}           \operatorname {Gr}_n^{\delta (W,L)}\mathrm {R}\Gamma (U_\bullet ,\mathbb {Q})           &\underset {(7.1.6.5)}{\simeq } \bigoplus _m \operatorname {Gr}_{n+m}^W \mathrm {R}\Gamma (U_m,\mathbb {Q})[-m]         \\&\simeq  \bigoplus _m \mathrm {R}\Gamma (\widetilde {Y}_m^{n+m},\varepsilon _\mathbb {Q}^{n+m}[-n-m])[-m]         \\&= \bigoplus _m \mathrm {R}\Gamma (\widetilde {Y}_m^{n+m},\varepsilon _\mathbb {Q}^{n+m})[-n-2m].         \end {aligned}       

The E_1 page of the corresponding spectral sequence is the sum of the groups of form \operatorname {H}^p(\widetilde {Y}_q^r,\varepsilon _\mathbb {Q}^r);
this contributes to the \operatorname {H}^{p+q+r}(U_\bullet );
it corresponds to \operatorname {Gr}_a^{\delta (W,F)} for p+2r=(p+q+r)+a:

1277hodge-theory-iii-8.1.19.1hodge-theory-iii-8.1.19.1.xmlEquation8.1.19.1           {}_{\delta (W,L)}E_1^{-a,b}           = \bigoplus _{\substack {p+2r=b\\q-r=-a}} \operatorname {H}^p(\widetilde {Y}_q^r,\varepsilon _\mathbb {Q}^r)           \Rightarrow  \operatorname {H}^{-a+b}(U_\bullet ,\mathbb {Q}).         \tag{8.1.19.1}

fr:mainmatter.fr:p:
If K' and K'' are mixed Hodge A-complexes, then then their product K'\otimes  K'', defined by
           \begin {aligned}         (K'\otimes  K'')_A         &= K'_A\overset {\mathrm {L}}{\otimes } K''_A       \\((K'\otimes  K'')_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)         &= (K'_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W')\otimes (K''_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W'')       \\((K'\otimes  K'')_\mathbb {C},W,F)         &= (K'_\mathbb {C},W',F')\otimes (K''_\mathbb {C},W'',F'')       \end {aligned}     
    is again a mixed Hodge A-complex.

In , the filtration \operatorname {Dec}_1(W) () plays a more important role than W. We have
1272hodge-theory-iii-8.1.16.1hodge-theory-iii-8.1.16.1.xmlEquation8.1.16.1 \operatorname {Dec}(\delta (W,L)) = \operatorname {Dec}_1(W) \qquad \text {on }\mathrm {s} K \tag{8.1.16.1}
and can be rewritten as
1273hodge-theory-iii-8.1.16.2hodge-theory-iii-8.1.16.2.xmlEquation8.1.16.2 \operatorname {Gr}_L(\operatorname {H}(K),\operatorname {Dec}_1(W,F)) = ({}_LE_\infty ,W,F). \tag{8.1.16.2}
In the same spirit, we note that, with the notation of , \mathrm {R}\Gamma \big ( \mathrm {R} j_*\mathbb {Z}, (\mathrm {R} j_*\mathbb {Q},\operatorname {Dec} W), (\Omega _X^\bullet (\log D),\operatorname {Dec} W,F) \big ) depends only on U, and not on the choice of compactification X.

html:ol.html:li:
The filtration \delta (W,L) of \mathrm {R}\Gamma \mathrm {R} j_*\mathbb {Q}\simeq \mathrm {R}\Gamma (U_\bullet ,\mathbb {Q}).
With the notation of , we have
         \begin {aligned}           \operatorname {Gr}_n^{\delta (W,L)}\mathrm {R}\Gamma (U_\bullet ,\mathbb {Q})           &\underset {(7.1.6.5)}{\simeq } \bigoplus _m \operatorname {Gr}_{n+m}^W \mathrm {R}\Gamma (U_m,\mathbb {Q})[-m]         \\&\simeq  \bigoplus _m \mathrm {R}\Gamma (\widetilde {Y}_m^{n+m},\varepsilon _\mathbb {Q}^{n+m}[-n-m])[-m]         \\&= \bigoplus _m \mathrm {R}\Gamma (\widetilde {Y}_m^{n+m},\varepsilon _\mathbb {Q}^{n+m})[-n-2m].         \end {aligned}       

The E_1 page of the corresponding spectral sequence is the sum of the groups of form \operatorname {H}^p(\widetilde {Y}_q^r,\varepsilon _\mathbb {Q}^r);
this contributes to the \operatorname {H}^{p+q+r}(U_\bullet );
it corresponds to \operatorname {Gr}_a^{\delta (W,F)} for p+2r=(p+q+r)+a:

1277hodge-theory-iii-8.1.19.1hodge-theory-iii-8.1.19.1.xmlEquation8.1.19.1           {}_{\delta (W,L)}E_1^{-a,b}           = \bigoplus _{\substack {p+2r=b\\q-r=-a}} \operatorname {H}^p(\widetilde {Y}_q^r,\varepsilon _\mathbb {Q}^r)           \Rightarrow  \operatorname {H}^{-a+b}(U_\bullet ,\mathbb {Q}).         \tag{8.1.19.1}

fr:mainmatter.fr:p:
In , we gave an introduction to the yoga that underlies  and the present article .
  However,  and  are logically independent of ;
  they also do not contain all the results that were stated in .

In the case of a complete singular algebraic variety X, the fundamental idea is to use the resolution of singularities to replace X by a simplicial system of smooth projective schemes
\usepackage {amsmath} \usepackage {amssymb} \usepackage {tikz-cd} \begin {tikzcd} X_\bullet = \Big (\ldots \quad X_2 \ar [r] \ar [r,shift left=4] \ar [r,shift right=4] & X_1 \ar [r,shift left=2] \ar [r,shift right=2] \ar [l,shift right=2] \ar [l,shift left=2] & X_0\Big ) \ar [l] \end {tikzcd}
(or, as we will say, by a smooth projective simplicial scheme). The results of and allow us to find such simplicial schemes X_\bullet that have, in a suitable sense, the same cohomology as X. We can then "express", via a spectral sequence, the cohomology of X in terms of the cohomology of the X_n. Following the general principles of , we can then, on each X_n, use classical Hodge theory, and obtain an induced mixed Hodge structure on the cohomology of X.

Let K be a complex of cosimplicial sheaves on S, endowed with an increasing filtration W.
    We define the diagonal filtration \delta (W,L) of W and L to be the increasing filtration of \mathrm {s} K given by

    2734hodge-theory-iii-7.1.6.1hodge-theory-iii-7.1.6.1.xmlEquation7.1.6.1         \begin {aligned}           \delta (W,L)_n(\mathrm {s} K)           &= \bigoplus _{p,q} W_{n+p}(K^{p,q})         \\&= \sum _p \mathrm {s}(W_{n+p}(K)) \cap  L^p(\mathrm {s} K).         \end {aligned}       \tag{7.1.6.1}

The functor (K,W)\mapsto (\mathrm {s} K,\delta (W,L)) sends filtered quasi-isomorphisms to filtered quasi-isomorphisms, and defines

    2736hodge-theory-iii-7.1.6.3hodge-theory-iii-7.1.6.3.xmlEquation7.1.6.3         \begin {aligned}           (s,\delta )           \colon  \mathrm {D}^+\mathrm {F}(S_\bullet )           &\to  \mathrm {D}^+\mathrm {F}(S)         \\(K,W)           &\mapsto  (\mathrm {s} K,\delta (W,L))         \end {aligned}       \tag{7.1.6.3}       

    whence, by composition with ,

    2737hodge-theory-iii-7.1.6.4hodge-theory-iii-7.1.6.4.xmlEquation7.1.6.4         \begin {aligned}           (\mathrm {R}\Gamma ,\delta (-,L))           \colon  \mathrm {D}^+\mathrm {F}(X_\bullet )           &\to  \mathrm {D}^+\mathrm {F}(S)         \\(K,W)           &\mapsto  (\mathrm {R}\Gamma  K,\delta (W,L)).         \end {aligned}       \tag{7.1.6.4}       

    From , we see that

    2738hodge-theory-iii-7.1.6.5hodge-theory-iii-7.1.6.5.xmlEquation7.1.6.5         \operatorname {Gr}_n^{\delta (W,L)}         = \bigoplus _p \mathrm {R}{a_p}_*(\operatorname {Gr}_{n+p}^W K)[-p].       \tag{7.1.6.5}

A DG mixed Hodge A-complex K consists of:






        a complex K_A\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+(A\mathsf {-mod}_{\mathsf {DG}^+});





        a filtered complex (K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}},W)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F((A\otimes \mathbb {Q})\mathsf {-mod}_{\mathsf {DG}^+}), and is isomorphism K_A\otimes \mathbb {Q}\simeq  K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}} in \mathrm {D}^+;





        a bifiltered complex (K_\mathbb {C},F,W)\in \operatorname {Ob}\mathrm {D}^+F_2(\mathbb {C}\mathsf {-mod}_{\mathsf {DG}^+}), and an isomorphism K_{A\otimes \mathbb {Q}}\otimes \mathbb {C}\simeq  K_\mathbb {C} in \mathrm {D}^+F.
